  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a CAR T cell is affected by the tumour microenvironment as it enters a solid tumour. A central CAR T cell is surrounded by both cellular and non-cellular components, with multiple inhibitory arrows pointing toward it, emphasizing that many parts of the microenvironment can suppress or limit CAR T-cell activity.\n\nAmong the cellular elements, the image shows tumour cells packed densely at the top, endothelial cells forming a barrier-like structure at the left, and several immunosuppressive cell types around the CAR T cell, including MDSCs, TAMs, Treg cells, and CAFs. These neighboring cells appear to act as obstacles or sources of suppressive signals. The endothelial layer and dense tumour mass also suggest physical barriers to CAR T-cell infiltration.\n\nThe lower part highlights non-cellular influences, including extracellular matrix (ECM), cytokines, chemokines, metabolites, and broader tumour microenvironment cues. A short list indicates conditions such as hypoxia, acidification, nutrient scarcity, and free radicals, all of which seem to contribute to dysfunction or reduced effectiveness.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that CAR T-cell therapy in solid tumours is challenged not just by tumour cells themselves, but by a complex network of stromal, immune, structural, and metabolic factors.",
